<section id="getting-started">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-8 pb-3 border-b border-grey-lighter">Getting started</h2>

    <div class="space-y-10 max-w-3xl">
        <div class="Story-warning">
            <strong>Read this page, then delete it.</strong> It belongs to the starter kit, not to
            your project — which is why it is marked <em>waiting</em>: it waits on the one thing the
            theme cannot do for you. Delete
            <code class="font-mono text-sm">components/Toolkit/getting-started.html.twig</code>
            and the page goes, navigation entry included — then drop its two lines from
            <code class="font-mono text-sm">ComponentStatus</code> and
            <code class="font-mono text-sm">ToolkitController::SECTIONS</code>, which stay behind
            harmlessly until you do.
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
            <p class="text-grey-darker">
                The toolkit renders every component of the theme, one page per component, from the
                stories it finds on disk. It only answers while the kernel runs in debug: a live shop
                returns a 404, so nothing here reaches a customer.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-grey-darker">Adding a story</h3>
            <p class="text-grey-darker">
                Drop a <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-grey-lightest px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">toolkit.html.twig</code>
                next to the component it shows, for instance in
                <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-grey-lightest px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">components/Molecules/Button/</code>.
                It is picked up on the next request: there is nothing to register. The folder path
                becomes the page name and its URL.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-grey-darker">Saying where a story stands</h3>
            <p class="text-grey-darker">
                Statuses live in one file,
                <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-grey-lightest px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">src/Toolkit/ComponentStatus.php</code>,
                keyed by the story path under <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-grey-lightest px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">components/</code>.
                The list ships empty: a story that is not in it shows nothing and claims nothing.
                <strong>It is not a backlog</strong> — only a way to say what has been settled.
            </p>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-grey-lighter divide-y divide-grey-lightest">
                <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-4">
                    <span class="Toolkit-statusBadge Toolkit-statusBadge--ready">ready</span>
                    <p class="text-sm text-grey-darker">
                        Handled on our side and open to review — not reviewed yet. Shown as a check
                        mark in the navigation, like the Welcome page.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-4">
                    <span class="Toolkit-statusBadge Toolkit-statusBadge--waiting">waiting</span>
                    <p class="text-sm text-grey-darker">
                        Stopped on something that is not the theme's to decide. A status cannot say
                        <em>what</em> is awaited, so the story carries a banner that does — the one
                        at the top of this page.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="flex items-start gap-4 p-4">
                    <span class="Toolkit-statusBadge">hidden</span>
                    <p class="text-sm text-grey-darker">
                        Will never be integrated: the story is dropped from the toolkit entirely. The
                        files stay, and so does the component's CSS import — removing it while a call
                        site remains would leave the component rendering unstyled.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-grey-darker">Previewing at a width</h3>
            <p class="text-grey-darker">
                The selector above the navigation renders the current page in an iframe at a chosen
                breakpoint. The width is kept while you move from one component to another, so a
                whole set can be reviewed at one size.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
